1
00:00:03,540 --> 00:00:25,617
Namo tassa bhagavato hara hatu samma sambuddhasa Namo tassa bhagavato hara hatu samma sambuddhasa Namo tassa bhagavato

2
00:00:25,968 --> 00:00:30,274
Parahato Sama Sambuddhasa.

3
00:00:30,795 --> 00:00:34,960
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

4
00:00:37,524 --> 00:00:43,594
So this is the inaugural talk around verses of the Dhammapada.

5
00:00:44,454 --> 00:00:47,659
And I've chosen what is probably the most famous of them all.

6
00:00:48,981 --> 00:00:54,270
I shall chant it in Pali first, just to give it that Eastern flavor.

7
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:22,840
as usually translated as hatreds never cease through hatred in this world through love alone they cease this is an eternal law so this is basically a statement of non-violence

8
00:01:24,305 --> 00:01:28,490
Now with each of these Dhammapada verses, there comes a little story.

9
00:01:29,212 --> 00:01:38,927
So I'm very fortunate, I have this book written by Sri Dhammananda, who was a Sri Lankan monk in Malaysia.

10
00:01:39,106 --> 00:01:39,828
He's died now.

11
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:48,100
And what he's done for us very kindly is he's reduced the stories to something fairly manageable.

12
00:01:48,742 --> 00:01:52,287
So I shall read this as a sort of an example of

13
00:01:52,772 --> 00:01:59,701
the way they would have thought or the way it was built up from perhaps a smaller original occasion.

14
00:02:01,584 --> 00:02:04,927
So it's a bitter feud through various existences.

15
00:02:05,748 --> 00:02:08,752
Now, once there lived a householder whose wife was barren.

16
00:02:09,713 --> 00:02:21,008
Being unable to bear a child and afraid that she would be mistreated by her husband and her mother-in-law, she herself selected and arranged for her husband to marry another woman.

17
00:02:22,134 --> 00:02:31,566
But on two occasions, as soon as she knew the second wife was pregnant, the barren wife gave her food mixed with drugs, causing her to have miscarriages.

18
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:39,735
On her third pregnancy, the fruitful wife kept it to herself without informing the barren wife.

19
00:02:40,276 --> 00:02:45,061
But when the latter came to know of it, she again caused a miscarriage.

20
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:49,205
Eventually, the second wife died in childbirth.

21
00:02:50,215 --> 00:02:58,646
Before her death, the unfortunate woman was filled with hatred and vowed vengeance on the barren wife and her future offspring.

22
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:00,949
Thus, the feud was started.

23
00:03:02,872 --> 00:03:16,110
Among their later existences, the two were born as a hen and a cat, a doe and a leopardess, and finally as the daughter of a nobleman in Savarty and an ogress.

24
00:03:17,652 --> 00:03:18,854
One day, the demon

25
00:03:19,358 --> 00:03:23,822
was in hot pursuit of the noble bird's daughter and her baby.

26
00:03:25,022 --> 00:03:34,211
When the lady heard that the Buddha was giving a religious discourse at the Jetavana monastery, she fled to him and placed her son at his feet for protection.

27
00:03:35,091 --> 00:03:38,514
The demon was thus prevented from entering the monastery.

28
00:03:39,495 --> 00:03:44,199
She was later called in and both Lady and Ogress were admonished by the Buddha.

29
00:03:45,259 --> 00:03:48,622
The Buddha told them about their past feud as rival wives

30
00:03:48,838 --> 00:03:57,086
and how they had been harboring hatred towards each other and killing each other's offspring through their various lifetimes.

31
00:03:58,889 --> 00:04:08,298
They were made to see that hatred could only cause more hatred and it could only cease through friendship, understanding and goodwill.

32
00:04:09,378 --> 00:04:15,504
Both realized their mistakes and on the admonition of the Buddha made their peace with each other.

33
00:04:16,447 --> 00:04:20,992
The Buddha then requested the woman to hand over her son to the ogres.

34
00:04:22,274 --> 00:04:32,348
Fearing for the safety of her son, she hesitated, but because of her devotion and confidence in the enlightened one, she handed over her son as directed.

35
00:04:32,408 --> 00:04:36,435
The child was warmly received by the ogres.

36
00:04:36,875 --> 00:04:44,766
After kissing and caressing the child tenderly, like her own very son, the ogres handed over the child back to his mother.

37
00:04:45,673 --> 00:05:13,521
as a result of this episode there was much goodwill on both sides so i don't know maybe there was a kernel incident there where a desperate husband maybe went to the buddha and said my two wives are at each other all day uh can you help and uh and then it built up from there an aggressive course is a sort of uh nasty nasty female shrek i suppose um

38
00:05:13,906 --> 00:05:24,596
But the story is basically about what happens when you react with hatred or anger to somebody else who's being angry or hateful.

39
00:05:25,959 --> 00:05:30,083
Now, what we have to do is convince ourselves that this is true.

40
00:05:30,543 --> 00:05:33,165
And I think the place to start is within ourselves.

41
00:05:34,086 --> 00:05:42,276
So if you think about what happens within us when somebody is angry with us or

42
00:05:42,408 --> 00:05:44,649
has a hateful attitude towards us.

43
00:05:45,571 --> 00:05:53,019
See, the response is immediately, isn't it, to react with that anger, to react with hatred.

44
00:05:53,839 --> 00:06:02,769
And you can see that this is just going to create more hatred and more anger because the other person now has something to chew on.

45
00:06:03,411 --> 00:06:09,057
And as they respond with more hatred and anger, so we respond with more hatred and anger.

46
00:06:09,425 --> 00:06:13,610
So that's a never ending process, which is just going to lead to a forest fire.

47
00:06:15,132 --> 00:06:21,500
And it's really catching that within us that often for no reason, we might just dislike somebody.

48
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:26,365
And that manifests in the way we look at them, the way we actually talk to them.

49
00:06:26,766 --> 00:06:37,660
And then we wonder why they're coming back to us, not recognizing that inside us, we have a very hateful attitude towards this person.

50
00:06:38,095 --> 00:06:41,879
It could be because of their political stance, their religious stance.

51
00:06:41,899 --> 00:06:43,019
It could be for any reason.

52
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:46,343
It could be just the way they look sometimes.

53
00:06:47,144 --> 00:06:53,271
And hatred is a sort of generalized attitude that passes through time.

54
00:06:53,932 --> 00:06:58,096
Anger, of course, is just a flare-up which tends to die away.

55
00:06:58,117 --> 00:07:04,244
Of course, you can bring it back to mind over and over again, you know, when we're daydreaming.

56
00:07:05,524 --> 00:07:07,427
But basically, these two things,

57
00:07:07,728 --> 00:07:19,822
these two mental heart reactions or something that we build up within ourselves, they're looking for something to really chew on.

58
00:07:20,141 --> 00:07:28,492
When the other person actually reacts to us with anger or with further hatred, then we ourselves find a reason to just continue.

59
00:07:28,672 --> 00:07:30,574
And so the feud begins.

60
00:07:31,535 --> 00:07:36,620
Now, there's an incident in the Buddha's life

61
00:07:37,242 --> 00:07:42,569
where the dastardly Devadatta set a wild elephant on him.

62
00:07:43,709 --> 00:07:49,918
Devadatta wanted to reform the order, to go back to its original ascetic practice.

63
00:07:50,038 --> 00:08:06,338
Rag rogues live in just a shelter made from twigs or trees and a banana leaf put over it, really sort of go right back to those early ascetic days.

64
00:08:06,723 --> 00:08:16,735
And, of course, the Buddha wouldn't accept that because the people who were ordained then just weren't really of that caliber.

65
00:08:17,516 --> 00:08:21,240
So he set this wild elephant on the Buddha.

66
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:25,245
Now, the Buddha, when he saw the elephant coming, raised his hand.

67
00:08:25,365 --> 00:08:28,750
So it's known as the abhaya posture, the fearless posture.

68
00:08:28,769 --> 00:08:36,139
Now, that's the first thing that we have to engender towards somebody who's hateful or who's angry with us.

69
00:08:36,506 --> 00:08:45,799
Somehow there has to be a feeling that they cannot destroy you, they cannot overwhelm you.

70
00:08:46,380 --> 00:08:49,184
There has to be that sort of internal strength within us.

71
00:08:50,086 --> 00:08:57,317
Now that might not be there, in which case we'll react in a way which is undermining ourselves.

72
00:08:57,998 --> 00:09:06,109
We'll either run or hide or in some way give in to the person who's being angry with us.

73
00:09:06,307 --> 00:09:08,291
you know, this is a learning process.

74
00:09:08,851 --> 00:09:22,711
And the thing is to build up a certain righteousness within ourselves so that we can stand steady within ourselves, a sort of an assertiveness about ourselves within the world.

75
00:09:23,292 --> 00:09:26,636
And that's difficult, you know, because, well, it's difficult.

76
00:09:27,798 --> 00:09:31,323
So the elephant comes towards him and he holds his hand up.

77
00:09:31,344 --> 00:09:35,909
Now, this hand is sort of also a way of him

78
00:09:36,177 --> 00:09:42,966
expressing his love towards the elephant, a non-violent love towards the elephant, asking the elephant to calm down.

79
00:09:43,787 --> 00:09:47,672
So it's a feeling he's sending out, a sort of beam of love.

80
00:09:48,111 --> 00:09:53,778
And the elephant does slow down and finally pays obeisance.

81
00:09:53,798 --> 00:10:00,648
Now, whether we want to believe these stories or not isn't really the point.

82
00:10:00,707 --> 00:10:06,014
The point is that we can overcome anger, other people's anger, other people's hatred,

83
00:10:06,433 --> 00:10:11,561
by non-reactivity and this idea of goodwill.

84
00:10:12,182 --> 00:10:16,788
Friendliness might not be there, but definitely we can have goodwill.

85
00:10:17,509 --> 00:10:36,254
Now, when somebody comes to us with that attitude of hatred and anger, it's because something is unhappy within them and they are expressing that outwards as an aggressive mental state, an aggressive mood.

86
00:10:37,113 --> 00:10:39,375
So it's a case of recognizing that.

87
00:10:39,875 --> 00:10:43,519
So what might we do if somebody is like that towards us?

88
00:10:44,559 --> 00:10:52,145
Well, we might be quick enough to envisage this hand so that we're receiving the emotional state, right?

89
00:10:52,166 --> 00:10:53,586
And that's being taken care of.

90
00:10:54,148 --> 00:11:06,798
And even though we might feel inside us a certain anger growing or a certain fear growing, it's as though the hand is also guarding us against further reactions

91
00:11:07,201 --> 00:11:11,687
because it's holding the other person at a distance.

92
00:11:12,467 --> 00:11:32,196
Now, in order to prevent us falling into the emotion that we're engendering in ourselves, if we lift our attention and pay attention to what they're saying, in other words, to put the attention on the ear rather than the heart, then of course, what we're doing is we're being compassionate.

93
00:11:32,255 --> 00:11:37,123
We're actually taking a position that maybe

94
00:11:37,524 --> 00:11:44,375
If I can get in touch with the hurt that sits behind their hatred or their anger, it might undermine it.

95
00:11:45,856 --> 00:11:50,683
That's an act of compassion, an act of trying to understand where the person is coming from.

96
00:11:51,664 --> 00:11:57,293
And hopefully the other person will suddenly recognize that actually you're listening to them.

97
00:11:57,894 --> 00:12:07,126
And that often undermines a person's anger and hatred, that finally, you know, you're actually paying attention to what their problem is.

98
00:12:07,528 --> 00:12:11,434
And that often, as I say, that often just assuages the whole situation.

99
00:12:11,794 --> 00:12:16,121
And then through discussion, et cetera, there can be a healing of the situation.

100
00:12:17,583 --> 00:12:26,476
And that's definitely one way that you can approach a situation where a person is angry or is hateful towards you.

101
00:12:28,419 --> 00:12:34,107
So I think we've been through it there, I think.

102
00:12:35,850 --> 00:12:36,051
Yeah.

103
00:12:36,908 --> 00:12:45,520
So we have this story of the ogres and these three qualities of friendship, understanding, you see, that's the compassion, and goodwill.

104
00:12:46,542 --> 00:12:49,846
And we have the story of the Buddha overcoming the elephant.

105
00:12:50,607 --> 00:12:58,900
So here we have some stories to support us, but also through our own internal investigation.

106
00:12:59,301 --> 00:13:00,503
That's the way we did.

107
00:13:01,158 --> 00:13:07,447
When we're angry and the other person's angry, before we know it, we're into some sort of rage.

108
00:13:08,188 --> 00:13:23,432
So this whole attitude of non-violence is of absolute importance in our lives so that we can heal our own hearts of that reactivity of anger and hatred.

109
00:13:24,153 --> 00:13:30,101
So if we go back to this little phrase, it's very simple, we can translate it as

110
00:13:30,672 --> 00:13:36,500
Hatred or anger never comes to end with the reaction of hatred and anger.

111
00:13:36,520 --> 00:13:39,504
Not in this world, not ever, not at any time.

112
00:13:39,565 --> 00:13:46,154
And it's only through goodwill, through understanding compassion that they come to cease.

113
00:13:46,796 --> 00:13:48,438
So this is an eternal law.

114
00:13:48,759 --> 00:13:51,543
This is an absolute law.

115
00:13:51,563 --> 00:13:57,152
I can only hope my words have been of some assistance, that they have not caused even greater confusion.

116
00:13:57,756 --> 00:14:05,657
And that by your devotion to the practice, you will be liberated from all suffering sooner rather than later.

